Is a connected socket's peer actually pinned by the kernel? Asked, not assumed.
==============================================================================
[  ok  ] TCP — data reaches the peer the mediator connected to — to-the-pinned-peer
[  ok  ] TCP — sendto() to another address does NOT deliver there — not delivered; sendto() accepted the call; connect() → EISCONN (Transport endpoint is already connected)
[  ok  ] TCP — the descriptor cannot be re-pointed at a second destination — sendto() accepted the call; connect() → EISCONN (Transport endpoint is already connected)
[  ok  ] UDP — data reaches the connected peer
[  ok  ] UDP — sendto() to another address DOES deliver: descriptor passing is unsafe — delivered — this is why every UDP path is relayed, not handed over
[  ok  ] UDP — inbound from a non-peer is dropped, so the receive half is pinned
==============================================================================
6/6 checks passed

Descriptor passing is safe for TCP and unsafe for UDP on this host, which is what
`notes/decision_network_capability.md` §5.1 is built on.
